{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CdVjtXEmpQn5oGgctftiT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is a question about CI, caching, and native addon builds. The user is asking whether a green CI run with a native addon build coming from layer cache actually tells them that the addon was rebuilt against the new ABI (Application Binary Interface), or if it just means a cached layer was reused.\n\nThis is a fundamentally important distinction:\n- If the native addon is coming from a cached layer, that typically means the build step didn't actually run—it just reused a previous build.\n- A lockfile bump might indicate dependency changes that would require the addon to be rebuilt against a new ABI.\n- A green CI run doesn't necessarily mean the addon was actually rebuilt; it could just mean the tests passed against the cached (potentially stale) addon.\n\nThe user is asking about observability and verification. They need a way to actually verify that the addon was rebuilt, not just that tests passed.\n\nKey points to consider:\n1. Layer caching in Docker/build systems is common and useful for speed, but it masks whether actual work happened\n2. A lockfile bump could indicate changes that need recompilation (e.g., Node.js version, build dependencies)\n3. Green tests against cached binaries don't prove the binaries are correct for the new ABI\n4. To verify a rebuild actually happened, you'd need to either:\n   - Disable caching for that layer\n   - Check build logs for compilation output\n   - Inspect the actual rebuild step with `--no-cache` flag\n   - Compare artifacts to confirm they changed\n   - Check timestamps or hash the binary\n\nThis is a practical DevOps/CI question. I should give them a direct answer about what the cached layer actually means and suggest ways to verify the rebuild actually happened.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6840,"cache_read_input_tokens":17536,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6840},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"REDACTED","uuid":"a0736e79-51ce-4042-b5d6-7d82888f6cf8","timestamp":"2026-07-29T04:45:38.816Z","request_id":"req_011CdVjtW277TCmfC1vy8SWQ"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CdVjtXEmpQn5oGgctftiT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A green run with a cached layer tells you only that the cached binary passed tests—not that it was rebuilt. The layer cache masks whether compilation actually happened, so you can't trust it for detecting ABI mismatches.\n\nTo verify the addon actually rebuilt:\n\n1. **Check the build log** — look for compiler output (gcc/clang invocations, object files being linked). Cached layers typically show \"Using cache\" or skip straight to the next layer.\n\n2. **Force a rebuild on this run** — pass `--no-cache` to the Docker build (or equivalent for your build system) to bypass the cache and confirm the addon recompiles and tests still pass.\n\n3. **Inspect the artifact hash** — before/after runs, compute the checksum of the `.node` binary. If it's identical, the cache was reused; if different, it actually rebuilt.\n\n4. **Disable caching for just the addon layer** — split your Dockerfile so the native build layer doesn't cache based on the lockfile alone. Make it cache-bust when you actually change dependencies (e.g., add a build arg that changes per commit).\n\nThe safest approach: run a one-off CI job with `--no-cache` after a lockfile bump to confirm the addon rebuilds and tests pass. Once you see that, you'll know the ABI compatibility is real rather than cached."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6840,"cache_read_input_tokens":17536,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6840},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"REDACTED","uuid":"a62e9778-78da-4692-96f5-04bba18c5c90","timestamp":"2026-07-29T04:45:42.799Z","request_id":"req_011CdVjtW277TCmfC1vy8SWQ"}
